From: Brady <brady@aioscilloscope.com>
Subject: of: reserved_mem: a zero-size region is a placeholder, not a failed reservation
Copyright (C) 2026 Olympian LED, Inc.

Device trees declare some reserved-memory regions with size 0 as
placeholders that the bootloader fills in when it uses them. An untouched
placeholder was reported as a failed reservation; skip zero-size regions
quietly.

---
diff --git a/drivers/of/of_reserved_mem.c b/drivers/of/of_reserved_mem.c
--- a/drivers/of/of_reserved_mem.c
+++ b/drivers/of/of_reserved_mem.c
@@ -129,7 +129,15 @@ static int __init __reserved_mem_reserve_reg(unsigned long node,
 		base = dt_mem_next_cell(dt_root_addr_cells, &prop);
 		size = dt_mem_next_cell(dt_root_size_cells, &prop);
 
-		if (size && early_init_dt_reserve_memory(base, size, nomap) == 0) {
+		if (!size) {
+			/*
+			 * A zero size is a placeholder that a bootloader fills in only when it
+			 * uses the region, e.g. the Rockchip U-Boot's drm-logo and drm-cubic-lut
+			 * after it has drawn a boot logo. Nothing to reserve, and not a failure.
+			 */
+			pr_debug("Reserved memory: node '%s' has a zero-size region, nothing reserved\n",
+				 uname);
+		} else if (early_init_dt_reserve_memory(base, size, nomap) == 0) {
 			/* Architecture specific contiguous memory fixup. */
 			if (of_flat_dt_is_compatible(node, "shared-dma-pool") &&
 			    of_get_flat_dt_prop(node, "reusable", NULL))
